best_response:
To find the specific value of "x", you can substitute the values of "a", "b", and "c" from the solution obtained using `solve` into the equation `x = a + 2b - c/3`. Here's how you can do it in MATLAB:

```matlab
% Get the symbolic solutions for 'a', 'b', and 'c'
sol = solve([eq1, eq2, eq3], [a, b, c]);

% Extract the numerical values of 'a', 'b', and 'c'
[A, B, C] = double(subs(sol, [a, b, c]));

% Calculate the value of 'x'
X = A + 2*B - C/3;

% Display the results
disp('Value of x:');
disp(X);
```

This will display the calculated value of "x" on the command window. Note that the `double()` function is used to convert the symbolic expressions obtained from `solve()` to their numerical equivalents.

Yes, you can achieve this by implementing a custom validation rule using the JIRA ScriptRunner plugin. The validation rule will check whether all items on the checklist are marked as done before allowing the issue to move to the next column. Here's an example script:

```groovy
import com.onresolve.plugins.scriptrunner.actions.ValidationResult
import com.onresolve.plugins.scriptrunner.actions.ValidationRule
import com.onresolve.plugins.scriptrunner.services.ProjectService

class ChecklistValidationRule implements ValidationRule {
    @Field('Checklist')
    String checklistName

    @Override
    def validate(transitionalContext) {
        def project = ProjectService.instance.getProject(transitionalContext.project)
        def issue = transitionalContext.issue
        def checklistItems = project.content.findAll { it instanceof com.atlassian.confluence.content.checksummary.CheckItem && it.name == checklistName }.collect { it.content as Map }
        boolean allChecked = true
        checklistItems.each { item ->
            def checked = false
            item.values().each { val ->
                if (val instanceof com.atlassian.confluence.content.checksummary.Checkbox) {
                    checked = checked || val.checked
                }
            }
            allChecked &= checked
        }
        return allChecked ? ValidationResult.newSuccess() : ValidationResult.newFailure("Please ensure that all items in the ${checklistName} checklist are checked.")
    }
}
```

Here's how to use this validation rule:

1. Install and configure the JIRA ScriptRunner plugin.
2. Create a new validation rule in the JIRA administration section and select the "ChecklistValidationRule" class you created earlier.
3. Configure the validation rule with the name of the checklist you want to validate.
4. Save and apply the validation rule to the relevant transition in your workflow.

This validation rule will prevent the issue from being moved to the next column if any item in the specified checklist is not checked. You can modify the script to suit your specific requirements, such as changing the error message or adding additional checks.

