To further optimize efficiency for handling a large amount of text, you can consider using text indexing techniques like inverted index or suffix trees. These data structures allow fast lookup and search operations for specific words or patterns in large text collections. Here's a high-level overview of how these techniques work:

Inverted Index:
An inverted index is a dictionary that maps each word to a list of line numbers (or document IDs) where it appears. By using this index, we can quickly retrieve all occurrences of a given word without having to scan through the entire text. In MATLAB, you can create an inverted index using the `invert` function, which returns a sparse matrix with nonzero entries corresponding to the positions of the searched word. You can then use the `find` function to extract the line numbers (or document IDs) where the word appears. Here's an example implementation:

```matlab
function uniqueWords = findUniqueWords(text1, text2)
    %Read text files and convert to lowercase
    text1 = lower(fileread('Text1.txt'));
    text2 = lower(fileread('Text2.txt'));

    %Create inverted indices for both texts
    idx1 = invert(strsplit(text1), ' ', 'sparse', true);
    idx2 = invert(strsplit(text2), ' ', 'sparse', true);

    %Find unique words in text1 that do not appear in text2
    uniqueWords = setdiff(idx1.row, idx2.row);
end
```

Suffix Trees:
A suffix tree is a data structure used to represent and search for all suffixes of a string efficiently. It allows us to perform operations like finding all occurrences of a pattern within a large text collection in linear time. Suffix trees can be constructed using the Ukkonen's algorithm or Morris-Pratt algorithm. However, they have a space complexity of O(n), where n is the length of the text. In practice, this may still be more efficient than scanning through the entire text multiple times when dealing with very long texts. Here's an example implementation using the Ukkonen's algorithm:

