Sure! Promises in JavaScript are like a contract between two parties (in this case, the code you write and the asynchronous operation you're waiting for). When you make an asynchronous request, such as fetching data from a server or reading a file, instead of blocking the main thread and making your app unresponsive, you can return a Promise.

A Promise is essentially an object that represents the eventual completion (or failure) of an asynchronous operation. It allows you to attach success and error callbacks, which will be called when the promise resolves (completes successfully) or rejects (fails). This way, you don't have to worry about managing multiple levels of callbacks, which can quickly become spaghetti code and hard to read/debug.

Here's a simple example:

```javascript
// Making an asynchronous request using Fetch API
const getData = () => {
  return fetch('https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts')
    .then(response => response.json())
    .catch(error => console.log('Error:', error));
};

// Consuming the Promise
getData()
  .then(data => {
    console.log('Success:', data);
  })
  .catch(error => {
    console.log('Error:', error);
  });
```

In the above example, `getData()` returns a Promise created by the `fetch()` method. We then chain `then()` and `catch()` methods on the returned Promise to handle its resolution or rejection respectively.

