One possible solution to make the generated planets less clustered would be to implement a distance constraint between each newly generated planet and all the existing ones. You can modify the `generateRandomPlanets` function as follows:

```javascript
const generateRandomPlanets = (numPlanets, minDistance) => {
  const planets = [];
  let attempts = 0;

  while (planets.length < numPlanets && attempts < 100) {
    attempts++;

    let hasCollision = false;

    // Generate initial position
    const position = [
      randomPosition(-30, 30),
      randomPosition(-30, 30),
      randomPosition(-30, 30),
    ];

    // Check for collisions with existing planets
    planets.forEach((p, i) => {
      const distance = Math.sqrt(
        Math.pow(position[0] - p.position[0], 2) +
          Math.pow(position[1] - p.position[1], 2) +
          Math.pow(position[2] - p.position[2], 2)
      );

      if (distance <= minDistance) {
        hasCollision = true;
        return;
      }
    });

    if (!hasCollision) {
      const planet = {
        position,
        size: randomSize(1, 5) * scale,
        color: randomColor(),
        ringSize: 0.2 * scale,
        ringColor: randomColor(),
        ringNoise: 0.3 * scale,
      };

      planets.push(planet);
    }
  }

  return planets;
};
```

In this modified version, we keep track of whether there is a collision with any existing planet by iterating over all of them after generating the initial position. If a collision occurs, we immediately flag it and exit the inner loop. This way, we avoid creating more planets until we find one that does not overlap with the others. Note that I also added an argument `minDistance` to specify the minimum distance between two planets, which can be adjusted depending on your preferences. With this change, the generated planets should be more spread out and less crowded

