Here's the implementation of `redis.js` following your requirements:

```javascript
const redis = require('redis');

class RedisClient {
  constructor() {
    this.client = new redis();
    this.client.on('error', (err) => {
      console.error(`Redis Client Error: ${err}`);
    });
  }

  async isAlive() {
    try {
      await this.client.ping();
      return true;
    } catch (err) {
      return false;
    }
  }

  async get(key) {
    return await this.client.get(key);
  }

  async set(key, value, duration) {
    await this.client.setex(key, duration, value);
  }

  async del(key) {
    await this.client.del(key);
  }
}

const redisClient = new RedisClient();
module.exports = redisClient;
```

The `RedisClient` constructor initializes a new `redis` client and listens to its 'error' event to log any errors encountered to the console. The `isAlive()`, `get()`, `set()`, and `del()` methods are asynchronous and utilize the native `redis` client methods with appropriate arguments. An exported `redisClient` instance is created at the bottom of the file. Note that I'm using `async/await` syntax here for better readability and clarity. However, you can replace them with callback-based style if needed.

